  "chunks": [
    {
      "chunk_type": "isnad",
      "arabic_text": "عِدَّةٌ مِنْ أَصْحَابِنَا عَنْ أَحْمَدَ بْنِ مُحَمَّدِ بْنِ عِيسَى عَنِ الْحُسَيْنِ بْنِ سَعِيدٍ عَنِ الْقَاسِمِ بْنِ سُلَيْمَانَ عَنْ جَرَّاحٍ الْمَدَائِنِيِّ عَنْ أَبِي عَبْدِ اللَّهِ ( عليه السلام ) أَنَّهُ قَالَ",
      "translations": {
        "en": "A number of our companions, from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Isa, from Al-Husayn ibn Saeed, from Al-Qasim ibn Sulayman, from Jarrah Al-Madainy, from Abu Abdillah (peace be upon him), that he said:"
      }
    }
  ,
    {
      "chunk_type": "body",
      "arabic_text": "لَا أَقْبَلُ شَهَادَةَ الْفَاسِقِ إِلَّا عَلَى نَفْسِهِ",
      "translations": {
        "en": "I do not accept the testimony of the immoral (fasiq) except against himself."
      }
    }
  ],
  "translations": {
    "en": {
      "summary": "Imam Abu Abdillah (peace be upon him) states that the testimony of a fasiq (a person who is morally corrupt or sinful but not a disbeliever) is generally rejected, with the specific exception that it is accepted when it is against himself (i.e., a confession).",
      "seo_question": "When is the testimony of a fasiq accepted in Shia jurisprudence?",
      "key_terms": {
        "شَهَادَة": "Testimony or witness testimony",
        "الْفَاسِق": "The immoral one; a person who commits sins but retains faith",
        "عقيدة Islam, often used in legal contexts to denote a witness whose testimony might be weakened or rejected"
